Title: LOW TIDE
Credit: A demo script by
Author: THEACTORPAGE
Draft date: 10/19/26
Notes: Made up for the tour. It has a few rough spots on purpose, so you can watch the tools find them.

INT. HARBOR OFFICE - NIGHT #1#

Rain on the glass. ANNA VOSS (40s), harbor detective, cold coffee, reads a file under a desk lamp. Slowly, she turns a page.

THE PHONE RINGS.

ANNA
Voss.

MR. DELL (V.O.)
They found the boat. Pier nine.

ANNA
(standing)
Who found it?

MR. DELL (V.O.)
Kids. Nobody on it. Don't go alone.

ANNA
I'm already gone.

She grabs her coat. The file stays open on the desk: a photo of a grinning kid in a wool cap. TOMMY O'NEIL, 19.

CUT TO:

EXT. PIER NINE - CONTINUOUS #2#

Wind. A rusted trawler, the MARY KATE, knocks against the pilings. OFFICER RUIZ (30s) holds a flashlight on her.

RUIZ
Detective. You're gonna want to see this.

ANNA
What is it? Tell me now, because I have been up since four and I am not walking onto that boat without knowing what I am walking onto.

RUIZ
(beat)
It's the O'Neil boat. Engine's cold. The lines were cut, not untied.

Anna crouches. Slowly she lifts a rope end into the light. Clean. A knife did that.

MRS. O'NEIL (O.S.)
Where is he?

Anna turns, slowly. MRS. O'NEIL, 60s, in a nightgown and work boots, stands at the end of the pier.

ANNA
Ma'am, stay there.

MRS. O'NEIL
That's my boat. That's my son's boat.

ANNA
Ruiz. Get her a coat. Get her home. I'll be there in an hour.

INT./EXT. ANNA'S CAR - MOVING - LATER #3#

Anna drives. The wipers can't keep up.

ANNA
(into phone)
Dell. The lines were cut. Somebody wanted that boat loose.

MR. DELL (V.O.)
Or the kid did.

ANNA
Why would a kid cut his own boat loose?

MR. DELL (V.O.)
Ask his mother. Gently.

ANNA
I'm always gentle.

MR. DELL (V.O.)
You are never gentle.

She almost smiles. Hangs up. Slowly, the harbor lights slide past in the rain.

INT. O'NEIL KITCHEN - NIGHT #4#

A small kitchen that smells of coffee and diesel. Two mugs. Mrs. O'Neil sits at the table in Ruiz's coat. Anna stands by the stove.

ANNA
When did you see him last?

MRS. O'NEIL
Supper. He ate standing up. He always eats standing up, like he's late for something.

ANNA
Was he?

MRS. O'NEIL
He's nineteen. Everything's late.

ANNA
Did he owe anybody? Money, a favor.

MRS. O'NEIL
You think he's in some kind of trouble.

ANNA
I think somebody cut that boat loose with a knife. I'd like to know who.

MRS. O'NEIL
(a long beat)
He wanted to sell it. His father's boat. I said over my dead body.

ANNA
And what did he say?

MRS. O'NEIL
He said that could be arranged. He was laughing. He was laughing when he said it.

ANNA
Mrs. O'Neil. Where would he go if he didn't want to be found?

MRS. O'NEIL
The boathouse. His father's. Nobody's been in it in six years.

EXT. SEAWALL - DAWN #5#

First light, flat and grey. Gulls. FATHER KEANE (70s) walks the seawall with a thermos, the way he does every morning. Anna falls in beside him.

FATHER KEANE
His mother called me at three. You look like you haven't slept.

ANNA
Did Tommy come to you?

FATHER KEANE
You know I can't tell you what's said to me.

ANNA
I'm not asking what he said. I'm asking if he's alive.

Father Keane stops. Slowly he pours coffee into the thermos cap and hands it to her.

FATHER KEANE
Drink that. Then go easy on him.

.THE BOATHOUSE - DAY #6#

Dust in the light. Nets hang from the rafters like old cobwebs. Anna slowly pushes the door open.

TOMMY O'NEIL sits on an overturned crate, a duffel bag at his feet. He doesn't run.

TOMMY
She sent you.

ANNA
Your boat sent me. You cut her loose.

TOMMY
I couldn't sell it. I couldn't keep it. I thought if the tide took it, nobody had to decide.

ANNA
Your mother thinks you're at the bottom of the harbor.

Tommy looks at the bag. Then at the door.

TOMMY
I was going to call her. From somewhere.

ANNA
Call her from here.

EXT. PIER NINE - DAY #7#

Sun on the water now. The MARY KATE is tied up again, with new line. Mrs. O'Neil waits at the end of the pier. Tommy walks toward her, slowly. Neither of them knows what to say first.

Anna watches from her car. Father Keane leans on the door.

FATHER KEAN
You were gentle.

ANNA
Don't tell Dell.

> FADE OUT.
